high PRIORITIES:

2. check marker v2 vs unlimited ocr vs mineru vs docling, slight decrease in accuracy but can move off from modal into cpu only server?
   MEASURED (see bench/parsers/README.md + cpu-parser-decision canvas). Unlimited-OCR out (GPU
   vLLM only). MinerU sub/sup corruption (68% markup noise on lecture decks) reproduces on Modal
   fast, Modal accurate AND their hosted platform on parse_method=auto/txt — issue raised upstream.
   parse_method=ocr CLEARS IT COMPLETELY (0% noise) and also fixes a broken font cmap in the same
   deck (U+FFFD chars, "Glycolysis S tep 1"). On the deck ocr is even faster than auto. Cost of ocr
   on clean born-digital docs: <=28% more time, 99.6% word-identical, same equations/headings; only
   risk is identifiers (llion@google.com -> 1lion@google.com).
   VLM CAN do OCR too: first accurate+ocr 10p try died at 542s (Modal HTTP gateway, not the VLM).
   Retried after snapshot restore: 3 pages in 2.86s GPU / 10 pages in 8.38s GPU, 0% noise.
   DONE: both Modal warmups + request fallbacks + EVO_PARSE_METHOD default are now `ocr`. Redeploy
   so the GPU snapshot is rebuilt with OCR sidecars inside it.
   CPU VM for fast: YES, but not by lifting MinerU. MinerU pipeline+ocr on 4 vCPU did the lecture
   deck in 54.9s / 0.20 pg/s (clean, 0% noise) vs 10.8s on the L4 (~5×) vs marker --disable_ocr
   22.5s. Marker 2 has NO Paddle/Rapid/Easy OCR plugin — OcrBuilder is wired to Surya VLM. The
   cheap OCR is RapidOCR (PP-OCRv6-small onnx, same family as Paddle) *next to* marker, not inside
   it: per-page router + marker --disable_ocr + RapidOCR on flagged pages = 0.27 pg/s composed.
   NOTE: auto returns byte-identical output to txt on that deck, i.e. auto silently picks the broken
   path whenever a text layer exists. Do not rely on auto.
2c. DONE: EVO_PARSE_METHOD default is `ocr` (config, docker-compose, .env.example). parse_method
   is already inside artifact_identity(), so a flip re-parses instead of serving cached corrupt
   zips. Tests still pin auto in conftest so cassettes do not need a re-record.
   Winner for fast: marker v2 `fast --disable_ocr` (1.10 pg/s warm on 4 vCPU, heading depth 4,
   equations) + a pypdfium2 per-page text-density router (17 ms/page) + RapidOCR ONNX on the flagged
   pages only. Composed 0.27 pg/s warm, 3.4x MinerU-on-CPU, recovers the most text of anything
   measured. Do NOT use marker's own OCR on CPU: surya 2 is a Qwen-VL served through llama.cpp at
   6.1 tok/s and it runs away past 2,000 tokens on a dense scan.
   Still open before building: worst-case latency ceiling for a 100-page upload, whether `accurate`
   becomes marker-balanced-on-GPU or is deleted, and concurrent uploads per box.
2b. FIXED: retrieval/chunking.py `chunk_content_list` branched on text/table/equation/image with no
   else, silently dropping every other block type. Real content lost: `list` (stores text under
   `list_items` — this was EVERY citation in every paper), `chart` (dropped from chunking AND from
   captioning, since parse/figures.py `select_figures` also required type == "image"), `header`
   (slide titles, and a book's "Contents"), `page_footnote`. Now split into _IMAGE_TYPES /
   _BODY_TEXT_TYPES / _LIST_TYPES / _FURNITURE_TYPES, with unrecognised types counted and logged.
   `header` is body text on purpose, NOT a heading: it has no text_level and a running header would
   otherwise overwrite section_path on every page of a book. Also fixed the new caption prompt
   emitting its "context provided below:" preamble when there is no context (dangling colon invites
   hallucination). 4 new tests, test-catalog.md updated.
